# config/Kconfig.development — Debug probes, trace ring, verbose logging, panic
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  Developer-facing options; should generally be off
# in production defconfigs.

menu "Development & Debug"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TCP trace probes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "TCP per-segment trace probes"
    default TCP_TRACE_OFF
    help
      Inserts kprintln! trace points at key TCP state transitions
      (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, FIN, RST, retransmit).  Useful for
      debugging the sensor-pump E2E path (#39).
      Has measurable performance impact — disable in production.

    config TCP_TRACE_OFF
        bool "Off (no trace output)"
        help
          Default.  Zero overhead on the hot TCP path.

    config TCP_TRACE_QEMU_ONLY
        bool "QEMU only (enabled when BOARD_QEMU)"
        help
          Trace probes active only under QEMU.  Automatically
          disabled in VF2/K1 builds by the build.rs feature guard.
          Good for CI debugging without affecting hardware builds.

    config TCP_TRACE_ALWAYS
        bool "Always (real hardware + QEMU)"
        help
          All builds include trace probes.  Use only for deep
          hardware bring-up debugging — performance impact is real
          (~3-5% slower TCP throughput per segment due to UART output).

endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WCET reporting
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "WCET violation reporting"
    default WCET_REPORT_NEVER
    help
      Controls when the WCET subsystem reports accumulated violation
      statistics.

    config WCET_REPORT_NEVER
        bool "Never (production default)"
        help
          WCET records violations in an in-memory counter only.
          No periodic output — avoids timing perturbation.

    config WCET_REPORT_ON_SHUTDOWN
        bool "On shutdown / panic"
        help
          Dumps accumulated violation counts to UART when the kernel
          exits or panics.  Useful for end-of-run post-mortem.

    config WCET_REPORT_PERIODIC
        bool "Periodically (every N scheduler ticks)"
        help
          Dumps WCET stats at regular intervals.  Adds UART output
          overhead proportional to reporting frequency.

endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Panic action
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Panic action"
    default PANIC_HALT
    help
      What the kernel does after printing the panic message.

    config PANIC_HALT
        bool "Halt (infinite WFI loop)"
        help
          Kernel spins in WFI forever after panic.  Safe default —
          no automatic restart, operator must intervene.

    config PANIC_REBOOT
        bool "Reboot after delay"
        help
          Kernel reboots after a fixed delay (was PANIC_REBOOT_DELAY_MS; removed 2026-09-26, V2.6 — never consumed).
          Allows reading the panic message before the reset.
          Useful for unattended field deployments where a stuck robot
          is worse than an automatically-recovering one.

    config PANIC_DUMP_HALT
        bool "Dump register state then halt"
        help
          Dumps full CPU register file (all GPRs + CSRs) to UART
          before entering WFI.  Maximum debug information;
          largest output.  Use during active debugging only.

endchoice

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Panic policy (RFC-0052 §5, RT7)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Panic policy"
    default PANIC_POLICY_CONTAIN if ROBOT_DRONE
    default PANIC_POLICY_RESET
    help
      Which kernel panics stop the machine. The panic action above applies
      to every panic that takes the reset path.

      Ground robots reset (halt or reboot) on any kernel panic. A flying
      robot with armed ESCs must not: a motor stop in flight is the failure.
      The Robot drone profile (ROBOT_DRONE) therefore defaults to Contain.

    config PANIC_POLICY_RESET
        bool "Reset (every panic: safe state, then halt or reboot)"
        help
          Every kernel panic stops all actuators (lock-free), sets the global
          panic flag (every other hart halts at its next tick and stops
          feeding the watchdog), writes the crash record (RAM pstore and
          /fat/CRASH.LOG) and takes the panic action. The behaviour before
          RT7.

    config PANIC_POLICY_CONTAIN
        bool "Contain (park the culprit task; the control loop keeps running)"
        help
          "Warn, don't kill." A panic in a non-safety kernel task is
          contained when its context passes the predicate in
          crates/core/common/src/panic_policy.rs: not in an interrupt
          handler, no SpinLock held by the hart, interrupts were enabled, no
          PiMutex held by the task, not a second panic, no hart already on
          the reset path, and the task is neither idle, nor ring 3 in a
          system call, nor a safety task (rt-motor, flight-ctrl). The task is
          parked (it exits with status 134 and its resources are released by
          the ordinary exit path); the global panic flag is NOT set, the
          actuators are NOT stopped, the other harts keep running and the
          crash counter (boot-loop detection) is not raised. The record still
          goes to the RAM pstore and /fat/CRASH.LOG, marked CONTAINED. The
          flight controller then flies its own Land and, after
          PANIC_CONTAIN_LAND_MS, Disarm; it never resets.

          Every panic that fails the predicate takes the reset path, as
          under Reset, and prints the reason. A fault on the control loop
          itself is one of those: the reset path stops feeding the watchdog
          and the external ESC/FC failsafe is the backstop.

          Cost (QEMU defconfigs, release, measured 2026-10-03): kernel
          .text +1742 B on riscv64 and +2128 B on aarch64 over Reset (the
          contain path and flight-ctrl's Land/Disarm sequence). Reset
          itself grew +1326 B / +2176 B over the tree before RT7 (the
          predicate captured on every panic, the safety-task registry,
          the PiMutex held count). Every PiMutex acquisition and release
          does one more atomic add on a 64-entry per-TID table (+6
          instructions each in riscv64 try_acquire and in the guard's release).

endchoice

config PANIC_CONTAIN_LAND_MS
    int "Contained panic: Land duration before Disarm (ms)"
    range 100 600000
    default 5000
    help
      After a contained kernel panic the flight controller (if armed) flies
      Land for this long, then disarms. There is no touchdown detection in
      the flight controller, so the descent is time-bounded: set it to the
      worst-case time to descend from the vehicle's operating altitude at
      the Land throttle. Read only when PANIC_POLICY_CONTAIN is set.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kernel console log level
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Kernel console log level"
    default LOG_LEVEL_WARN if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default LOG_LEVEL_WARN if PROFILE_EDGE
    default LOG_LEVEL_WARN if PROFILE_FLEET
    default LOG_LEVEL_WARN
    help
      Which kernel log lines are compiled in, Linux numbering (err 3,
      warning 4, info 6, debug 7). A line above the level is not compiled
      at all: no format string, no formatting code, no call
      (crates/drivers/sys/src/uart.rs, `kerr!`/`kwarn!`/`kinfo!`/
      `kdebug!`; a plain `kprintln!` is info).

      Every deployment profile defaults to warn: errors, security
      refusals and safety warnings only. The QEMU development configs
      (config/defconfigs/qemu.config, qemu-aarch64.config), which the
      gate builds, select debug so every gate row reads its lines.

      Not affected by the level: the console output of the shell and of
      ring-3 programs, the panic report, and the durable records (flight
      recorder, /fat/CRASH.LOG, pstore).

    config LOG_LEVEL_ERR
        bool "err: errors only"

    config LOG_LEVEL_WARN
        bool "warn: errors, security refusals, safety warnings"

    config LOG_LEVEL_INFO
        bool "info: also boot progress and status lines"

    config LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG
        bool "debug: every kernel line"

endchoice

config LOG_LEVEL
    int
    default 3 if LOG_LEVEL_ERR
    default 4 if LOG_LEVEL_WARN
    default 6 if LOG_LEVEL_INFO
    default 7 if LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Masked-window tracer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config LAT_TRACE
    bool "Interrupts-masked / preemption-disabled window tracer"
    default n
    help
      Records, per hart, the longest window with interrupts masked and
      the longest with preemption disabled, each with the call site that
      opened it and the one that closed it, plus the longest window per
      opening site. Read out at /proc/latency. The analogue of Linux's
      irqsoff/preemptoff tracers, for measuring a worst-case latency
      budget.

      Off (default): the hooks, the tables and the procfs entry are not
      compiled at all. Maps to the kernel cargo feature `lat-trace`
      (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py). On: every interrupt mask/unmask through
      the arch-api `Interrupts` trait, every preemption-counter 0<->1
      transition and every trap entry/exit reads the counter once.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kernel event tracer (wave 15)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config KTRACE
    bool "Kernel event tracer, read live from ring 3"
    default y if LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG
    default n
    help
      Per-CPU lock-free rings of 32-byte event records (scheduler,
      interrupts, syscalls, IPC, page faults, process life cycle, and
      the LAT_TRACE maxima) in one shared-memory region that a ring-3
      reader holding Cap<Trace> maps and drains while the kernel runs
      (userspace/services/tracectl). The kernel is the only producer of
      each ring, the reader the only consumer; a record touches one
      cache line, takes no lock and divides nothing.

      Off (default in every deployment profile, which build at log
      level warn): no tracepoint, no ring, no region is compiled; every
      tracepoint folds to zero instructions, and SYS_TRACE_CTL_TYPED
      answers -ENOSYS. On (default in the QEMU development configs,
      which select LOG_LEVEL_DEBUG): each compiled-in tracepoint costs
      its runtime-mask test while its class is masked off, and the
      record path while it records (see KTRACE_BOOT_MASK). The
      per-tracepoint and per-event instruction counts are measured in
      ARCHITECTURE.md ("Tracing").

config KTRACE_RING_ENTRIES
    int "Trace records per CPU ring (power of two)"
    range 64 8192
    default 256 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 1024
    help
      Records each CPU's ring holds before the policy applies (a full
      ring drops the newest record, or overwrites the oldest: see the
      policy choice). Read only with KTRACE. Must be a power of two (the
      slot is index & (entries - 1); the build refuses anything else).

      RAM: entries x 32 B x online CPUs, plus 64 B of header and 128 B
      per ring, in one kernel-owned shared-memory region, allocated at
      boot. The region is capped at MAX_SHM_PAGES (64 pages, 256 KiB):
      when the online CPUs times this value does not fit, the boot
      halves the entries until it does and prints a warning. 1024 on 4
      CPUs is 129 KiB. The same on both ISAs: the record size and the
      region cap do not depend on the ISA. Embedded boards default to
      256 (32 KiB on 4 CPUs).

      Raise it when a reader that wakes rarely reports drops; lower it
      to save RAM. The number of instructions per event does not change.

config KTRACE_BOOT_MASK
    hex "Trace classes recording from boot (runtime mask)"
    range 0x0 0x7f
    default 0x0
    help
      The runtime class mask the tracer starts with; tracectl changes it
      with SYS_TRACE_CTL_TYPED (TRACE_OP_SET_MASK). Bit n is class n:
      0 sched, 1 irq, 2 syscall, 3 ipc, 4 fault, 5 proc, 6 lat. Bits of
      classes not compiled in are ignored. Read only with KTRACE.

      0 (default): nothing records until a reader asks, so a tracer
      that is compiled in but idle costs only the mask test per
      tracepoint (a load, an and and a branch). Set 0x7f with
      KTRACE_POLICY_OVERWRITE for a flight recorder: the panic path
      dumps each CPU's last records.

choice
    prompt "Trace ring policy when a ring is full"
    default KTRACE_POLICY_DROP
    help
      What a full ring does with the next record. Read only with KTRACE.

    config KTRACE_POLICY_DROP
        bool "Drop the newest record and count it"
        help
          Default. The record is refused and the ring's drop counter
          (in the shared region) counts it; the reader sees every
          record it was given exactly once and never a torn one. The
          producer never writes a slot the reader has not released.

    config KTRACE_POLICY_OVERWRITE
        bool "Overwrite the oldest record (flight recorder)"
        help
          The newest records always land. Each record is first marked
          in progress, then written, then published (one more store and
          a store-store fence per record); the reader re-checks every
          record after copying it and counts the ones overwritten under
          it as lost. With no reader attached a full ring stays full,
          so every record also re-reads the reader's tail and stores the
          ring's overwrite count (a load and a store more). Use with
          KTRACE_BOOT_MASK for a black box whose last events survive to
          the panic dump.

endchoice

choice
    prompt "Trace timestamp source"
    default KTRACE_TS_TIMEBASE
    help
      The counter each record's timestamp reads. Read only with KTRACE.

    config KTRACE_TS_TIMEBASE
        bool "The monotonic timebase (the clock vDSO's)"
        help
          Default. rdtime on riscv64, CNTVCT_EL0 on aarch64: the counter
          the vDSO clock reads, the same on every CPU, at the rate the
          region header publishes (10 MHz on QEMU riscv64 virt, the
          live CNTFRQ_EL0 on aarch64), so records from different CPUs
          merge into one timeline. One instruction per record.

    config KTRACE_TS_CYCLES
        bool "The CPU cycle counter"
        help
          rdcycle on riscv64, PMCCNTR_EL0 on aarch64 (enabled at boot):
          finer, but per CPU (not comparable across CPUs), its rate is
          not published (the header says 0) and it stops in WFI on
          some cores. For measuring short sections on one CPU. Booted on
          QEMU, both ISAs (rdcycle is readable from S-mode under OpenSBI;
          each CPU starts PMCCNTR_EL0 itself); on a board, check the
          firmware leaves the counter readable (mcounteren.CY) first.

endchoice

config KTRACE_STATIC_KEYS
    bool "Tracepoints as patched branches (static keys)"
    depends on KTRACE && !NO_MMU
    default y if ARCH_RISCV64
    default y if ARCH_AARCH64
    default n if ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Each tracepoint is one instruction in the kernel text, a branch to
      the class's mask test while the class may record and a nop while it
      is masked off: a masked tracepoint costs one nop instead of the mask
      load, and, and branch (4 instructions). Like Linux's static keys.
      Changing the class mask (SYS_TRACE_CTL_TYPED, Cap<Trace>) rewrites
      every site of the changed classes: rare and slow by design.

      The text stays read-execute. A site is rewritten only through
      azos_mm::text_poke, which maps the page's frame at a temporary,
      never-executable alias, writes the word, unmaps the alias with a
      shootdown on every CPU and synchronises every CPU's instruction
      fetch (riscv64: fence.i and an SBI remote fence.i; aarch64: IC
      IALLUIS, DSB, ISB). Each site is a single naturally aligned 32-bit
      instruction swapped for another (nop and jal/b), which aarch64
      allows concurrently (CMODX) and riscv64 Linux relies on.

      Every site is linked as the branch, so a kernel that cannot patch
      (this off, no-MMU, or no free alias address at boot) keeps the
      4-instruction mask test and stays correct. Costs 24 bytes of table
      per site. Off: the mask test at every tracepoint.

config KTRACE_CLASS_SCHED
    bool "Trace class: scheduler (switch, wakeup)"
    depends on KTRACE
    default y
    help
      Two tracepoints: every context switch (do_schedule) and every
      wakeup of a sleeping task. Off: neither is compiled.

config KTRACE_CLASS_IRQ
    bool "Trace class: interrupts (entry, exit)"
    depends on KTRACE
    default y
    help
      An entry and an exit record around every external and timer
      interrupt the kernel dispatches, both ISAs. Off: not compiled.

config KTRACE_CLASS_SYSCALL
    bool "Trace class: system calls (entry, exit, seccomp denial)"
    depends on KTRACE
    default y
    help
      Entry and exit of every syscall, both ISAs, and every seccomp
      denial. The heaviest class by event rate: off, the syscall path
      carries no tracepoint at all (the vsbench syscall-floor lane is
      the measure). Replaces SYSCALL_TRACE.

config KTRACE_CLASS_IPC
    bool "Trace class: fast IPC (call, reply)"
    depends on KTRACE
    default y
    help
      A record for every fast-IPC call and reply. Off: not compiled.

config KTRACE_CLASS_FAULT
    bool "Trace class: page faults"
    depends on KTRACE
    default y
    help
      A record for every page fault the trap handlers see, both ISAs,
      with the address and the cause. Off: not compiled.

config KTRACE_CLASS_PROC
    bool "Trace class: process life cycle (spawn, exit, signal)"
    depends on KTRACE
    default y
    help
      A record when a task is created, when one exits (with its code)
      and when a signal is delivered to a Linux task. Off: not compiled.

config KTRACE_CLASS_LAT
    bool "Trace class: masked-window maxima (LAT_TRACE)"
    depends on KTRACE && LAT_TRACE
    default y
    help
      A record each time LAT_TRACE measures a new longest
      interrupts-masked or preemption-disabled window on a CPU, with
      its length and the two call sites. Costs nothing outside those
      new maxima. Needs LAT_TRACE.

config SWITCH_CENSUS
    bool "Census of the yield-and-switch path, phase by phase"
    default n
    help
      Measurement only (crates/core/sched/src/swcensus.rs). Times each
      phase of a yield that switches (trap and user mode, syscall
      dispatch, the pick, the re-queue, the context-saving gate, the
      accounting, context_switch, the return) and counts switches into
      each task slot; SYS_TASKINFO prints the per-switch averages since
      its previous call as `[SW-CENSUS]` lines at log level debug when at
      least SWITCH_CENSUS_DUMP_MIN switches happened in between. The clock
      is rdcycle (riscv64) or cntvct_el0 (aarch64): under QEMU -icount
      both count guest instructions. Meaningful at one hart (one set of
      globals for all harts).

      Off (default): not compiled, zero instructions. On: about ten
      instructions per phase boundary (eight per switch) and 800 bytes of
      counters.
      Maps to the kernel cargo feature `switch-census`
      (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py).

config SWITCH_CENSUS_DUMP_MIN
    int "Fewest switches that make a census dump worth printing"
    range 1 1000000000
    default 1000
    help
      SWITCH_CENSUS prints at a SYS_TASKINFO only when at least this many
      switches happened since the previous one, so the windows of a
      benchmark print and the bookkeeping calls between them do not.
      No cost while SWITCH_CENSUS is off; read once per SYS_TASKINFO when
      on. Raise it when a workload calls SYS_TASKINFO often.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In-kernel test registry (wave 15)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config KTEST
    bool "In-kernel tests (ktest): run every registered test at boot, then power off"
    default n
    help
      Compiles every `ktest!` test (crates/core/ktest) and the runner
      (kernel/src/ktest.rs). After boot init, before the secondary harts
      wake and before any task runs, the runner executes each test in name
      order and prints TAP on the console (`1..N`, `ok i - name`,
      `not ok i - name # reason`), then a summary line, then powers the
      machine off. A panicking test is reported `not ok` by the panic
      handler and ends the run (the kernel does not unwind).

      Off (default in every mode, development included): no test, no entry
      in `.azos_ktest`, no runner; zero bytes and zero instructions. It is
      not on in the QEMU development configs because the runner ends the
      boot: it is a test kernel, built for the gate's `ktest` rows. On: the
      tests' code and 24 bytes of `.rodata` per test, and the boot stops
      after the run. Maps to the kernel cargo feature `ktest`
      (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py).

endmenu # Development & Debug
